From 2b91eedad291ba9e3f6342f0313a4676d97aabd9 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Rob Currie <rob@example.invalid>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 15:17:57 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat: a class the file records under another checksum is read
 as the program has it

A collection written member by member is read straight into objects the
program has already made, so ROOT reads its items by the layout the class
has in memory - bases and all - and the record then ends somewhere other
than its byte count said: TBufferFile::CheckByteCount reports the bytes
taken against the record's own count and reads on from its end, after
TStreamerInfo::CreateReadMemberWiseActions' warning that the checksum moved
under the same version. interp now does both, through the registry the
declaring layer publishes layouts into (interp.declare).

Only a class the file records at the version in memory under a different
checksum is read that way; one recorded at another version is read by that
version's layout, as a record of it anywhere else is - reading every
declared class in memory is what made a v1 Holder's record end four bytes
short. The two messages count their bytes from just past the record's byte
count word, which is what ROOT's own numbers show: a record whose word says
48 is reported as "56 instead of 44" (roottest's mixedBase_v1).

Nothing publishes a layout yet: pyroot/core/declared.py must call
interp.declare when it describes a class, which is where the remaining
roottest evolution tests are blocked (see the round-9 report).

tests: both branches inside _divergence's, _byte_count's and
_read_by_memory's reports are reached - a class nobody declares, one whose
checksum moved, one that agrees, and a record that ended short.
